WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.037
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.337 --> 00:00:08.630
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:15.542
Unit fifteen: Corporate Power. In this unit, you will discuss the influence of large companies.

4
00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:17.981
market dominance.

5
00:00:18.681 --> 00:00:23.083
market dominance means control of most of a market by one company.

6
00:00:23.383 --> 00:00:26.335
The company's market dominance worries regulators.

7
00:00:27.035 --> 00:00:30.268
Your turn. Say: market dominance.

8
00:00:32.968 --> 00:00:34.317
market dominance.

9
00:00:35.317 --> 00:00:36.480
antitrust.

10
00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:42.001
antitrust means related to laws that prevent unfair business monopolies.

11
00:00:42.301 --> 00:00:45.601
The government launched an antitrust investigation.

12
00:00:46.301 --> 00:00:49.348
Your turn. Say: antitrust.

13
00:00:52.048 --> 00:00:53.223
antitrust.

14
00:00:54.217 --> 00:00:55.566
shareholder value.

15
00:00:56.266 --> 00:01:00.680
shareholder value means profit and returns for the owners of company shares.

16
00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:05.198
Some CEOs focus only on shareholder value.

17
00:01:05.898 --> 00:01:09.212
Your turn. Say: shareholder value.

18
00:01:11.912 --> 00:01:13.261
shareholder value.

19
00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.041
corporate tax avoidance.

20
00:01:16.741 --> 00:01:21.573
corporate tax avoidance means legal methods companies use to pay less tax.

21
00:01:21.873 --> 00:01:25.254
Corporate tax avoidance costs governments billions.

22
00:01:25.954 --> 00:01:29.651
Your turn. Say: corporate tax avoidance.

23
00:01:32.351 --> 00:01:33.979
corporate tax avoidance.

24
00:01:34.983 --> 00:01:36.170
conglomerate.

25
00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:42.179
conglomerate means a large company made up of many different businesses.

26
00:01:42.479 --> 00:01:46.684
The conglomerate owns banks, hotels and TV channels.

27
00:01:47.384 --> 00:01:50.501
Your turn. Say: conglomerate.

28
00:01:53.201 --> 00:01:54.318
conglomerate.

29
00:01:55.317 --> 00:01:56.155
merger.

30
00:01:56.855 --> 00:01:59.784
merger means when two companies join to become one.

31
00:02:00.084 --> 00:02:02.768
The merger created the country's largest bank.

32
00:02:03.468 --> 00:02:06.678
Your turn. Say: merger.

33
00:02:09.378 --> 00:02:10.100
merger.

34
00:02:11.117 --> 00:02:12.652
regulatory capture.

35
00:02:13.352 --> 00:02:19.810
regulatory capture means when regulators serve the industry they are supposed to control.

36
00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:23.630
Critics accuse the agency of regulatory capture.

37
00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:27.621
Your turn. Say: regulatory capture.

38
00:02:30.321 --> 00:02:31.879
regulatory capture.

39
00:02:32.883 --> 00:02:34.117
supply chain.

40
00:02:34.817 --> 00:02:39.719
supply chain means all the steps and companies involved in making a product.

41
00:02:40.019 --> 00:02:42.866
The pandemic disrupted global supply chains.

42
00:02:43.566 --> 00:02:47.158
Your turn. Say: supply chain.

43
00:02:49.858 --> 00:02:51.196
supply chain.

44
00:02:52.183 --> 00:02:54.392
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:54.692 --> 00:02:58.793
Rithy, did you read about the merger of the two biggest tech companies?

46
00:03:00.083 --> 00:03:04.884
Yes. The regulator warned that it would create too much market dominance.

47
00:03:06.183 --> 00:03:09.843
The CEO insisted that the merger would benefit consumers.

48
00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:15.776
He would say that, wouldn't he? Critics claimed it was only about shareholder value.

49
00:03:17.083 --> 00:03:19.907
There were also reports of regulatory capture.

50
00:03:21.217 --> 00:03:26.142
Apparently, an official admitted having met company lobbyists dozens of times.

51
00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:28.602
And tax?

52
00:03:29.917 --> 00:03:35.121
A journalist revealed that the conglomerate had paid almost no corporate tax for years.

53
00:03:36.417 --> 00:03:39.798
Grammar focus: Reported speech with nuance.

54
00:03:40.098 --> 00:03:46.393
At C1, choose reporting verbs that show attitude: insist, warn, claim and reveal.

55
00:03:46.693 --> 00:03:50.887
Admit and deny can be followed by having plus past participle.

56
00:03:51.187 --> 00:03:55.067
For example: The official admitted having met lobbyists.

57
00:03:55.367 --> 00:04:00.327
Choosing a precise reporting verb shows the speaker's attitude without extra words.

58
00:04:01.317 --> 00:04:04.155
Now read and listen. Too Big to Control?.

59
00:04:04.455 --> 00:04:10.854
A handful of global companies now influence what billions of people buy, read and watch.

60
00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:15.810
Supporters claim that large firms create jobs and drive innovation.

61
00:04:17.117 --> 00:04:22.471
Critics, however, warn that market dominance can lead to higher prices and less choice.

62
00:04:23.783 --> 00:04:29.614
Investigations have revealed that some conglomerates pay very little tax through complex structures.

63
00:04:30.917 --> 00:04:35.145
Antitrust authorities have the power to block mergers, but they rarely do.

64
00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:40.458
Some economists argue that regulatory capture weakens these authorities.

65
00:04:41.750 --> 00:04:46.397
Meanwhile, consumers are demanding greater transparency in supply chains.

66
00:04:47.683 --> 00:04:53.270
The question is no longer whether corporations are powerful, but how that power should be balanced.

67
00:04:54.583 --> 00:04:57.317
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:04:57.617 --> 00:05:01.114
When two companies join to become one, it is a blank.

69
00:05:01.414 --> 00:05:06.252
A, merger. B, supply chain. C, conglomerate.

70
00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:12.482
The answer is A: merger.

71
00:05:13.983 --> 00:05:16.807
The manager blank that the report was accurate.

72
00:05:17.107 --> 00:05:21.608
A, insisted. B, told. C, said us.

73
00:05:25.617 --> 00:05:28.348
The answer is A: insisted.

74
00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:31.699
He denied blank the documents.

75
00:05:31.999 --> 00:05:35.826
A, to take. B, having taken. C, take.

76
00:05:39.817 --> 00:05:42.861
The answer is B: having taken.

77
00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:47.708
When regulators serve the industry they control, it is blank.

78
00:05:48.008 --> 00:05:54.819
A, regulatory capture. B, antitrust. C, shareholder value.

79
00:05:58.817 --> 00:06:01.919
The answer is A: regulatory capture.

80
00:06:03.417 --> 00:06:06.647
According to the text, what are consumers demanding?

81
00:06:06.947 --> 00:06:13.491
A, lower taxes. B, transparency in supply chains. C, more mergers.

82
00:06:17.483 --> 00:06:21.677
The answer is B: transparency in supply chains.

83
00:06:23.183 --> 00:06:28.518
Now it's your turn. Part one: Do large corporations have too much power today?

84
00:06:33.818 --> 00:06:39.532
Part two: Report what supporters and critics say, then give your own conclusion.

85
00:06:45.832 --> 00:06:57.401
Supporters claim that big companies drive innovation, while critics warn that they avoid tax and limit competition. Personally, I'd argue that they need stronger regulation, especially regarding tax and data.

86
00:06:57.701 --> 00:07:00.548
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:06.362
Great work today! You learned eight new words and reported speech with nuance.

88
00:07:06.662 --> 00:07:10.672
Today's value is honesty. Always tell the truth.

89
00:07:10.972 --> 00:07:13.741
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

90
00:07:14.041 --> 00:07:17.297
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

